/* English strings of notes/circuits, harvested by tools/i18n.cjs (do not edit by hand: harvest again) */
VDI18N.add('en', 'notes/circuits', [
"Circuits: teaching notes",
"{#} to {#} minutes per mode; one class period for all three",
"Kirchhoff's rules hold in every circuit at every instant: currents into a junction equal currents out, and voltage changes around a loop add to zero.",
"Series parts share the battery voltage; parallel parts each get all of it. A real battery's terminal voltage ε − Ir falls as its current rises.",
"A capacitor's voltage and an inductor's current cannot jump; they change exponentially with τ = RC or L/R. An LC circuit oscillates at ω₀ = {#}/√(LC); R damps it, critically at R = {#}√(L/C). Charging a capacitor through any resistor stores half the battery's work and heats the resistor with the other half.",
"A diode passes current one way, with I growing tenfold for each {#} V or so; a junction transistor's small base current controls a collector current β times larger (I_C = βI_B), until the collector circuit cannot carry more and it saturates like a closed switch.",
"With a sine source, X_C = {#}/(ωC) and X_L = ωL set the current and its phase; a series RLC circuit resonates at f₀ = {#}/({#}π√(LC)) with half power width Δf = f₀/Q.",
"Students should know V = IR and Q = CV; the AC part needs sines and phasors. Switching between the three modes, <strong>DC</strong>, <strong>In time</strong> and <strong>AC</strong>, keeps the circuit when it has something to show there. Links ending #dc, #rc, #transients or #ac open a mode. The starter row (Ohm's law, Diode, LED colors, Rectifier, Transistor switch, Amplifier) opens with #ohm, #diode, #led, #rectifier, #transistor and #amplifier. Every part can be added to any circuit, so the demo is also an open circuit builder.",
"<strong>DC: Series bulbs, Parallel bulbs.</strong> Ask Q1 first. Both ammeters in series read {#} mA; <strong>Loop rule</strong> under Kirchhoff check gives +{#} − {#} − {#} = {#} V. In parallel, use <strong>Junction rule</strong>. Then <strong>Series vs parallel</strong> (Q2).",
"<strong>Internal resistance.</strong> Step R down with its value grip and watch the voltmeter fall (Q3). Close S1: {#} A, {#} V.",
"<strong>In time: RC charging.</strong> Ask the first part of Q4, then run it. The scope marks {#}τ to {#}τ; click S1 to discharge at any time. <strong>Flip mid way</strong> discharges from {#}%.",
"<strong>Camera flash.</strong> It charges in fast forward; then S1 flips by itself and the scope refits to the {#} ms flash (Q5).",
"<strong>RL rise and decay</strong>, <strong>LC oscillation</strong>, <strong>RLC damping</strong> (Q6, Q7). Change R2 with its value grip and watch ζ under Natural response.",
"<strong>AC: Capacitor only</strong>, <strong>Inductor only</strong> (Q8): the rotating phasors beside the scope project onto the traces. <strong>Below resonance</strong> shows the voltage phasors tip to tail (Q9).",
"<strong>At resonance</strong>, <strong>High Q vs low Q</strong>, <strong>Sweep</strong>. Then press <strong>In time</strong>: the same circuit is switched on at t = {#} and its current builds up over several cycles (Q10).",
"<strong>Diodes and transistors.</strong> <strong>Diode</strong>: change the resistor and watch the diode voltage barely move while the current changes by decades (Q11). <strong>LED colors</strong>: red lights first, blue needs the most voltage. <strong>Rectifier</strong>: the probe shows the smoothed output and its ripple. <strong>Transistor switch</strong>: raise R_B until the LED dims (Q12). <strong>Amplifier</strong>: the probe shows the collector swing, about ten times the input and inverted.",
"<strong>For a lab.</strong> Tick <strong>Blind: hide predictions and answers</strong> under <strong>Lab</strong>: it hides f₀, Δf, Q, τ, ζ and the like, so students measure them. In time, <strong>Step forward</strong> moves on by the chosen <strong>Step Δt</strong>; click or drag on the scope to place the time cursor, which reads every channel at that instant. <strong>Record reading</strong> fills a table in each mode, and <strong>Export to worksheet</strong> sends it to the matching student worksheet, with the circuit in the CSV header.",
"<strong>Q1.</strong> Predict: in Series bulbs the current leaves the battery's + terminal and reaches L1 before L2. Is L1 brighter than L2? Does the first ammeter read more than the second? Explain before you look.",
"<strong>Q2.</strong> Predict: in Series vs parallel all four bulbs are {#} Ω and both batteries are {#} V. Rank the four bulbs by brightness. What happens to the other bulb in each circuit if one bulb burns out? Check by deleting one bulb in each.",
"<strong>Q3.</strong> The battery in Internal resistance has ε = {#} V and r = {#} Ω. Find the terminal voltage and the power in the load for R = {#} Ω, {#} Ω and {#} Ω. Which load gets the most power, and what fraction of the EMF's power is that? Check each case.",
"<strong>Q4.</strong> Predict first: in RC charging, what are V_C, V_R and I the instant the switch connects the battery, and a long time later? Then, with ε = {#} V, R = {#} kΩ and C = {#} µF, find τ, the initial current, V_C at t = {#} s, and the time at which V_C reaches {#} V. Pause the demo to check.",
"<strong>Q5.</strong> In Camera flash (ε = {#} V, R1 = {#} kΩ, C1 = {#} µF, bulb {#} Ω), find the battery's work, the stored energy and the heat in R1 for a full charge. Would a larger R1 change the heat? Then find the peak power in the bulb when a full capacitor is switched to it, compared with the peak power in R1 at the start of charging.",
"<strong>Q6.</strong> In RL rise and decay (ε = {#} V, R1 = {#} Ω, L1 = {#} H), find τ and the final current. S1 flips at t = {#}τ. What is the inductor voltage just before and just after the flip, and why does it change sign while the current keeps its direction?",
"<strong>Q7.</strong> In LC oscillation (C = {#} µF charged to {#} V, L = {#} mH), find the period and the largest current. Then, for RLC damping, find the resistance for critical damping and the damping ratio ζ = (R/{#})√(C/L) for R2 = {#} Ω and {#} kΩ. Which of the three settles fastest, staying within {#}% of zero from then on? Check ζ under Natural response, and compare the traces.",
"<strong>Q8.</strong> Predict: in Capacitor only, at the instant the source voltage is at its positive peak, is the current at its peak, zero, or at its negative peak? Explain using q = Cv and i = dq/dt.",
"<strong>Q9.</strong> Below resonance has R = {#} Ω, L = {#} mH and C = {#} µF, driven at {#} kHz with V₀ = {#} V. Find X_L, X_C, |Z|, I_rms and the phase. The rms voltages across R, L and C add to more than the source's {#} V. Does that break the loop rule? Explain what does add up.",
"<strong>Q10.</strong> For the same parts find f₀, Q = ({#}/R)√(L/C) and the half power width, first for R = {#} Ω and then for R = {#} Ω. In High Q vs low Q (R = {#} Ω) press In time: the source is switched on at t = {#}. The transient decays as e^(−Rt/2L). How long is that decay time, and about how many cycles is it?",
"<strong>Q11.</strong> In Diode ({#} V battery), the current goes from {#} µA at R = {#} MΩ to {#} mA at {#} kΩ. By how much does the diode voltage change? Why is \"a silicon diode drops {#} to {#} V\" a good rule of thumb?",
"<strong>Q12.</strong> In Transistor switch ({#} V, R_C = {#} Ω, red LED, β = {#}), find I_B and I_C for R_B = {#} MΩ and for {#} kΩ, taking V_BE = {#} V. Why is the second I_C not {#} I_B?",
"<strong>Q13.</strong> Wall outlet: the outlet is {#} V rms at {#} Hz and the bulb is {#} Ω. Find the peak voltage, the rms current and the average power. Under Graphs pick Power in time: what are the largest and smallest values of p = vi, and how many times per second does p repeat?",
"<strong>Q14.</strong> Power factor: the load is {#} Ω in series with {#} mH on {#} V rms at {#} Hz. Find the power factor, the source current and the average power with S1 open. What capacitance across the load makes the power factor exactly {#}, and what is the source current then? Close S1 and check with C1 = {#} µF.",
"<strong>\"Current gets used up by the first bulb.\"</strong> The same current comes out of a part as goes in. What the part takes is energy, q ΔV per charge.",
"<strong>\"No current flows in a circuit with a capacitor.\"</strong> Charge flows onto one plate and off the other while the voltage changes. Only in the DC steady state is the current zero.",
"<strong>\"An inductor's voltage is set by its current.\"</strong> It is set by how fast the current changes, V = L dI/dt: a steady current gives none, a current forced to stop suddenly gives a huge one.",
"<strong>\"A transistor makes current.\"</strong> It controls it: the collector current comes from the battery, and the transistor sets how much flows, using the small base current.",
"<strong>\"The rms voltages around an AC loop must add to the source voltage.\"</strong> The instantaneous voltages add, at every instant, and so do the phasors. The rms values do not, because the voltages peak at different times.",
"One solver for every mode: modified nodal analysis with ideal wires, switches and meters. DC treats capacitors as open gaps, inductors and AC sources as plain wire.",
"In time, the circuit equations (I = dQ/dt, V = L dI/dt) and the energies are integrated exactly between switch events (matrix exponential). Where an ideal circuit would need an infinite current or voltage, a {#} mΩ contact resistance or {#} MΩ spark path stands in.",
"AC is the exact steady state from complex impedances, per source frequency plus the DC part. Meters read rms; the scope and phasors use peak values.",
"Bulbs are fixed resistors with no thermal lag (glow ∝ power, full at {#} W); a real filament's resistance rises as it heats.",
"Diodes, LEDs and transistors use real part data: a 1N4148 diode (Shockley equation with n = {#} and {#} Ω series resistance), catalog LEDs (forward voltage at {#} mA, n = {#} assumed) and a 2N3904 transistor (Ebers–Moll, β a setting, default {#}), all at {#} °C. DC solves them by Newton's method to {#}⁻¹⁰ V; AC uses the small signal model about the DC operating point; In time steps them with TR-BDF2 and adaptive steps (relative error {#}⁻⁶), so with them the time stepping is very accurate but not exact. Left out: breakdown, junction capacitance, the Early effect. Junction currents above {#} A are continued linearly, since a real part would have burned out.",
"The time cursor is exact in circuits without diodes or transistors: between stored samples the state is carried to the cursor time by the same matrix exponential, not drawn as a straight line."
]);
